{"id":"meaning-p2-adult","title":"Adaptive nulling","audience":"adults","blocks":[{"type":"subheading","html":"Steering away from noise"},{"type":"paragraph","html":"Adaptive antenna arrays cut interference by steering nulls, the directions they listen to least, toward the sources they want to ignore. The weights that shape the pattern are updated every two milliseconds.","sourceRefs":[{"passageId":"p1"}]},{"type":"paragraph","html":"In field trials the array improved the signal-to-noise ratio by eleven decibels with a single interferer and by seven with two.","sourceRefs":[{"passageId":"p2"}]},{"type":"paragraph","html":"Performance degrades with more than four interferers, because an array of four elements has only three degrees of freedom left once one is spent on the wanted direction.","sourceRefs":[{"passageId":"p3"}]},{"type":"paragraph","html":"Every trial ran in an anechoic chamber, and the array was never tested against a moving interferer, so outdoor performance is expected to fall off sooner.","sourceRefs":[{"passageId":"p4"}]}],"passages":[{"id":"p1","text":"Adaptive antenna arrays reduce interference by steering nulls toward unwanted sources. The method updates weights with a least-mean-squares rule every two milliseconds, which is fast enough for fixed interferers. Four patch elements were spaced at half a wavelength."},{"id":"p2","text":"In field trials the array improved the signal-to-noise ratio by eleven decibels with one interferer and by seven with two. The desired signal arrived at broadside in every trial, which is the easiest case for the array. Convergence took between forty and three hundred updates."},{"id":"p3","text":"The authors note that performance degrades when more than four interferers are present, because the array has only three degrees of freedom left once one is spent on the desired direction. In seventeen of forty trials the array reduced desired-signal gain by more than two decibels."},{"id":"p4","text":"All trials were run in an anechoic chamber. Multipath in a real building would present many more apparent sources, so the degradation is expected to appear at lower interferer counts outdoors. The array was not tested with moving interferers."}],"question":"What does paragraph 2 mean by steering nulls?","focusIndex":2,"expect":{"grounding":"story","cites":["b2"]}}
